  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Little change to the forecast reasoning with\nmedium range guidance continuing to converge on a solution for \nthe mid to late-week period, but notable differences remain. \nWhere guidance is fairly well clustered is with an amplified \nupper trough that will be shifting east out of the Central U.S. \nWhile there is still some potential for a cutoff low to develop,\nmost guidance have now trended towards more of a progressive \nand open wave as it moves through the Eastern U.S. Guidance \ndiffers on the timing, which leads to differences in \nprecipitation placement, temperatures, etc. But, most guidance \nis now centering on the best chance of showers and thunderstorms\ncoming Thursday and/or Friday. Of note, some guidance are \nshowing anomalous moisture and higher-than-normal instability \nfor early November. This would support the potential of some \nhigher rainfall rates and totals should this materialize. It may\neven support at least a marginal risk of strong to severe \nthunderstorms. While there is some synoptic support for this \npotential, machine learning guidance doesn't show that strong of\na signal. It's something worth keeping an eye on as the \nevolution of the upper level, and surface, pattern become more \nclear.\n\nThe other aspect of the forecast that appears to be coming more\nclear this week is the potential for above to well above normal\ntemperatures, especially Wednesday and Thursday. While record \nhighs may not be broken, warming low-level thicknesses in \nadvance of the approaching trough should support highs warming \ninto the 70s, with some low 80s possible inland. Meanwhile, \novernight lows are forecast to be closer to our normal highs for\nthis time of year. Some record warm lows may be in jeopardy. \nFor reference, normal highs are around 70 in early November, \nwith normal lows in the 40s inland and 50s along the coast.",
